\title{Every nonzero ring has a maximal ideal}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2023-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Let #{R} be a nonzero ring. Let #{(S, \leq)} be the set of all proper ideals of #{R} ordered by inclusion.}

\p{#{R} non-zero #{\implies \lbrace 0\rbrace  \in S \implies S} non-empty. If #{T \subset S} is a chain, then let #{J = \bigcup_{I \in T} I}.}

\p{If #{x, y \in J} then #{x \in I_1} and #{y \in I_2} for some #{I_1, I_2 \in T}. #{T} totally ordered #{\implies} either #{I_1 \subseteq I_2} or #{I_2 \subseteq I_1} #{\implies x + y \in J}. Also #{r \in R}, #{x \in J \implies rx \in J}. So #{J} is an ideal in #{R}. It is a proper ideal since #{1 \notin J}. Therefore #{J \in S} is an upper bound for #{T}.}

\p{Zorn’s Lemma shows #{S} has a maximal element, hence #{R} has a maximal ideal.}
